<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>false</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <WarnOn>3390;$(WarnOn)</WarnOn>
    <!-- 0.1.11 (2026-07-22): the Phase 94 pilot-5 lenient wave (c5e7863) landed in
         Column.fs + DataFrame.fs AFTER 0.1.10 was packed, so the feed's 0.1.10
         bytes predated the feature whose own commit message names that version.
         Additive — values-only columns, flat logical/comparison spellings, epoch
         timestamps — so consumers repin without source changes. Advancing rather
         than repacking 0.1.10 in place: this is the one package every domain
         consumes, so a same-version swap would give each machine a different
         contract depending only on when it last packed. -->
    <!-- 0.2.1 (2026-07-31): fuaran#665 — `Fuaran.Core.Row` + `RowCodec` (typed grid/chart
         row-source payload; the rows slot leaves the residual-"<opaque>" boundary).
         Additive — new type + module in Fuaran.Core.Wire; no existing surface moved. -->
    <!-- 0.3.0 (2026-08-18): Phase 101 — the relational algebra's closed-set asymmetries.
         `Transform` gains Intersect/Except; `JoinKind` Semi/Anti; `AggFn` CountDistinct;
         `WindowFn` DenseRank/CompetitionRank/NTile/CumulMax/CumulMin/RollingSum; `ScalarFn`
         Sqrt/Least/Greatest/IndexOf. Additive — every pre-existing pipeline encodes to
         byte-identical wire (the sole new field, a window step's "n", is emitted only for
         NTile), so consumers repin without source changes. MINOR rather than patch because
         STABILITY.md pins "adding a step kind is minor", and because an F# consumer that
         matches these DUs exhaustively will see new incomplete-match warnings. -->
    <!-- 0.4.0 (2026-08-20): `Fuaran.Core.Idl` joins the packed set — the IDL engine was
         IsPackable=false from its Phase 316 spike, so no domain workspace could consume it.
         Adding a package to the repo's published set is a contract change even though no
         existing package's surface moved: `dotnet pack Fuaran.Core.slnx` now emits a tenth
         nupkg, and a consumer that pins 0.4.0 gets an id that did not exist at 0.3.0.
         MINOR rather than patch on those grounds; purely additive for the nine existing
         packages, so consumers repin without source changes. See DECISIONS.md D14. -->
    <!-- 0.5.0 (2026-08-21): `Hash` gains the pinned pure SHA-256 — `sha256Hex` over a string,
         `sha256Bytes` / `sha256HexOfBytes` over arbitrary bytes, and the `utf8Bytes` encoder they
         hash through. Purely additive: `fnv1a` and `foldSep` are byte-identical and merely moved
         into their own file, so every stored content hash is unchanged and consumers repin without
         source changes. MINOR rather than patch because a new public function in a shipped module
         is a contract a consumer can pin against, and because this reverses a stated posture —
         STABILITY.md previously said no cryptographic hash ships in Core. See DECISIONS.md D15. -->
    <!-- 0.6.0 (2026-08-21): every FNV-1a in the spine routes its multiply through a split-half
         32-bit form so it computes true 32-bit FNV-1a on BOTH pipelines. Six implementations, not
         one: the canonical `Hash.fnv1a`, `OpStream.defaultHash` (the CHAIN hash), `Column`'s
         `Schema.fingerprint`, and three duplicates now deleted in favour of the canonical one.
         No public surface changed and
         .NET values are byte-for-byte unchanged — the pinned vectors enforce that — so a .NET
         consumer repins with no source change and no persisted digest moves. MINOR rather than
         patch because STABILITY.md makes these functions' VALUES the contract, and under Fable
         every value changes: the pre-0.6.0 transpiled multiply overflowed 2^53 and was not FNV-1a.
         A patch bump would tell a Fable consumer nothing observable moved, which is false. See
         DECISIONS.md D16 and the "Digest changes" entry in STABILITY.md. -->
    <!-- 0.7.0 (2026-08-21): the null-tolerant read. `Fuaran.Core.Wire.NullPolicy` +
         `Json.parseDetailedWithPolicy` / `parseWithPolicy` / `parseTolerantOfNull*` and
         `Decode.parseTolerantOfNull`, so a consumer can read a foreign document that spells
         absent members `null` without the wire model gaining one; plus the
         `Conformance.WireNullTolerance` vector family that pins it. Purely additive — the
         strict policy is the default, every pre-existing entry point is byte-identical under
         it (same errors, positions and messages), and nothing on the encode path moved, so
         consumers repin without source changes. MINOR rather than patch because a new public
         type and new public functions in a shipped module are contracts a consumer can pin
         against, and because a consumer matching `NullPolicy` is now possible at all. -->
    <!-- 0.8.0 (2026-08-21): the IDL engine splits — `Fuaran.Core.Idl` keeps the model, the codec,
         the sampler, the `idl.json` artifact and the sanitisation floor, and is now Fable-gated;
         a new `Fuaran.Core.Idl.Codegen` package takes the source emitters, `CodegenError`, the
         codegen trust boundary and the diff classifier. BREAKING for a consumer of
         `Fuaran.Core.Idl` 0.4.0-0.6.0 that called `Gen`, `Trust` or `Diff`: those types are still
         `Fuaran.Core.Idl.*` and still compile unchanged, but they arrive from a second package now,
         so the fix is one added PackageReference and no source edit. `Gen.sampleNodes` is the one
         genuine source change — it is `Sample.sampleNodes`, and it moved the other way, INTO the
         model half. MINOR rather than major under the pre-1.0 policy at the head of STABILITY.md.
         Additive for the nine non-IDL packages, which repin with no change at all. Eleven nupkgs
         where 0.7.0 emitted ten. See DECISIONS.md D17 and STABILITY.md "The IDL engine — two
         packages, two promises". -->
    <!-- 0.9.0 (2026-08-21): Phase 100 — the fold-confluence pack. `Fuaran.Core.OpStream.Dag` gains
         `Dag.reconcileMany` (the N-lane generalisation of `reconcile`, identical to it at N = 2),
         and `Fuaran.Core.Conformance` gains the `FoldConfluence` module — `laneFoldLaws` /
         `certifyFold` plus the pieces a domain drives directly when investigating a counterexample
         (`foldOnce`, `shrinkLanes`, `arrivalOrders`, `canonicalConflictReport`, `renderLanes`) and
         the two new public types `LaneFoldOutcome` / `LaneGen`. Purely additive: no existing
         function's behaviour, signature or emitted bytes moved, so consumers repin without source
         changes. MINOR rather than patch because new public types and functions in shipped packages
         are a contract a consumer can pin against — the same grounds as 0.5.0. See STABILITY.md
         "Fold-confluence pack: N-lane arrival-order invariance". -->
    <Version>0.9.0</Version>
    <Authors>Andrew J. Willshire</Authors>
    <Company>Diametrical Ltd</Company>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>Apache-2.0</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <Copyright>Copyright 2026 Diametrical Ltd</Copyright>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/fuaran-ui/fuaran-core</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/fuaran-ui/fuaran-core</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Fable source distribution. Every packable
       Core project ships its F# source + .fsproj under `fable/` in the package, so a
       Fable-compiled consumer (the Fuaran.UI client) compiles Core from source — alongside
       the `lib/` DLL for .NET. The standard dual-pack convention (Feliz / Thoth). The internal
       ProjectReferences become nuspec dependencies on pack; the dependency packages ship their
       own `fable/` source, so Fable resolves the whole Core graph transitively. -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(IsPackable)' == 'true'">
    <Content Include="$(MSBuildProjectFile)" PackagePath="fable\" />
    <Content Include="*.fs" PackagePath="fable\" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
